lastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n\nChronic Constipation Complications vs Infectious Enteritis:\n  Favours first:  bloating, feeling of incomplete evacuation, hard stools, headache, infrequent bowel movements\n  Favours second: bloody stool, dehydration, fatigue, fever, vomiting\n\nChronic Constipation Complications vs Adhesive Bowel Obstruction:\n  Favours first:  bloating, feeling of incomplete evacuation, hard stools, headache, infrequent bowel movements\n  Favours second: abdominal distention, blood in stool, dehydration, fever, green or yellow-green vomit\n\nChronic Constipation Complications vs Diabetic Gastroparesis:\n  Favours first:  bloating, feeling of incomplete evacuation, hard stools, headache, infrequent bowel movements\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, feeling full quickly when eating, heartburn, poor appetite, trouble controlling blood sugar\n\nVascular Insufficiency vs Infectious Enteritis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, bloody stool, dehydration, fatigue, fever\n\nVascular Insufficiency vs Adhesive Bowel Obstruction:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: abdominal distention, abdominal pain, blood in stool, dehydration, fever\n\nVascular Insufficiency vs Diabetic Gastroparesis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, feeling full quickly when eating, heartburn, nausea\n\nInfectious Enteritis vs Adhesive Bowel Obstruction:\n  Favours first:  bloody stool, fatigue, weight loss\n  Favours second: abdominal distention, blood in stool, green or yellow-green vomit, lethargy, obstipation\n\nInfectious Enteritis vs Diabetic Gastroparesis:\n  Favours first:  bloody stool, dehydration, fatigue, fever\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, feeling full quickly when eating, heartburn, poor appetite, trouble controlling blood sugar\n\nAdhesive Bowel Obstruction vs Diabetic Gastroparesis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal distention, blood in stool, dehydration, fever, green or yellow-green vomit\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, feeling full quickly when eating, heartburn, poor appetite, trouble controlling blood sugar\n\nRelated to Vascular Insufficiency: aortic valve insufficiency, chronic intestinal vascular insufficiency, congenital mitral valve insufficiency\n\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.69):]\n  Source: 2017 Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Infectious Diarrhea. > Differential Diagnosis\n  Pathogens, eg, Salmonella, Escherichia coli, Shigella, Campylobacter, Giardia lamblia, and Clostridium difficile may be potential causes in patients with atypical symptoms of viral gastroenteritis. Ot\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.71):]\n  Source: 2017 Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Infectious Diarrhea. > History and Physical\n  Acute gastroenteritis is defined by loose or watery diarrhea that consists of 3 or more bowel movements in a day. Other symptoms may include nausea, vomiting, fever, or abdominal pain.[3] Symptoms usu\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.79):]"}
