ledge Layer: coverage=50%, source=dxs]\n\nMechanical Bowel Obstruction vs Chronic Constipation Complications:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abdominal cramping, blood in stool, dark-colored urine, firm abdomen\n  Favours second: bloating, feeling of incomplete evacuation, hard stools, headache, infrequent bowel movements\n\nMechanical Bowel Obstruction vs Atherosclerotic Peripheral Artery Disease:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abdominal cramping, abdominal pain, blood in stool, dark-colored urine\n  Favours second: atrophy of the calf muscle, brittle toenails, changing skin color on legs, coldness in the lower leg or foot, elevation pallor of the leg\n\nChronic Constipation Complications vs Atherosclerotic Peripheral Artery Disease:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, bloating, feeling of incomplete evacuation, hard stools, headache\n  Favours second: atrophy of the calf muscle, brittle toenails, changing skin color on legs, coldness in the lower leg or foot, elevation pallor of the leg\n\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.63):]\n  Source: Management of chronic constipation in adults. > Toxicity\n  Laxative abuse is not uncommon and is found in patients with anorexia nervosa or bulimia nervosa and older patients who continue to use laxatives once started for constipation. It also includes patien\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.67):]\n  Source: American Gastroenterological Association medical position statement on constipation. > Indications\n  Constipation is a prevalent complaint encountered in clinical practice, affecting approximately 16% of all adults and 33% of adults 60 and older. There is no universally applicable definition of const\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Pathophysiologically, chronic constipation generally results from inadequate fiber or fluid intake or from disordered colonic transit or anorectal function. These result from neurogastroenterologic di\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.73):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  A small minority (probably <5%) of patients have severe or “intractable” constipation; about 25% have evacuation disorders. These are the patients most likely to require evaluation by gastroenterologi\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  constipation → fecal impaction → Mechanical Bowel Obstruction\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Assess for fecal impaction as a potential cause of mechanical bowel obstruction, given the patient's chronic constipation history\n  nausea and vomiting exacerbated with large fatty meals and alcohol → biliary dyskinesia → Biliary/Pancreatic Disorder\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for biliary dyskinesia or pancreatitis triggered by fatty meals/alcohol, which would support biliary/pancreatic disorder\n  poorly managed diabetes → diabetic autonomic neuropathy → Gastrointestinal Motility Disorder\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Investigate for diabetic autonomic neuropathy as an underlying cause of gastrointestinal motility disorder\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
